          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is the symptoms and abnormal findings chapter for, and when should a code from it be used instead of a disease code?",
          "answer": "Chapter 18 of ICD-10-CM (R00 to R99) covers symptoms, signs, and abnormal clinical and laboratory findings that are not classified elsewhere. It is used for the state a record is in before a diagnosis is made. (Source: *ICD-10-CM Chapter 18: symptoms, signs and abnormal findings*)\n\nAn R code is used when the record describes a symptom, sign, or abnormal result, and no more specific diagnosis has been established. Once a diagnosis is documented, the diagnosis code replaces the symptom code. There is one exception: a symptom that is not routinely associated with that diagnosis can be reported in addition, because it adds information the diagnosis does not. (Source: *Chapter 18*)\n\nSome examples from the same guidance:\n\n- A raised blood pressure reading, or several readings, without a diagnosis of hypertension is a finding and sits in Chapter 18. (Source: *Coding hypertension: I10*)\n- Fluid overload, swelling in the legs, or congestion in the lungs, documented without a heart failure diagnosis, are classified in Chapter 18. (Source: *Coding heart failure: I50*)\n- Blood sugar results that fall short of a diabetes diagnosis, such as prediabetes, are not coded to diabetes. They sit with the abnormal laboratory findings in Chapter 18. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11*)\n\nThe retrieved passages did not list every code in the chapter or explain the full set of sub-rules, so I can't give a complete list here. For a specific case, a trained medical coder or the official ICD-10-CM tabular list is the right reference.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
